{
  "assessment": "The source provides a complete experimental and modeling study of colloid filtration in a heterogeneous porous medium. It includes a controlled flow rate, a measured deposition profile and breakthrough curve, a stochastic A-CTRW model with explicit parameters (e.g., attachment rate k, critical distance d_c, pore size distribution parameters), and quantitative predictions. The evidence supports at least one numeric control (flow rate Q), numeric readouts (deposition profile a(x), BTC c(t)/c0), a quantitative mechanism (A-CTRW with flight/dive dynamics), and an objective source-grounded response within an explicit domain (distance x and time t). Thus, case_authoring_ready is true.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Filippo Miele et al., Flow-Induced Intermittent Transport Shapes Colloid Filtration in Complex Media, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/122c-f92l",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/122c-f92l",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2505.02689v1"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper develops a multiscale microfluidic experiment and an alternated continuous time random walk (A-CTRW) model to explain colloid filtration in a heterogeneous porous medium. It reports macroscopic deposition profiles (DP) and breakthrough curves (BTC), pore-scale particle tracking (PT) revealing flights and dives, and model predictions for DP and BTC. The model is constrained by experimentally measured parameters (pore size distribution, critical distance, attachment rate).",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "A_CTRW_filtration",
      "statement": "Colloid transport is modeled as a 1-D sequence of alternating flights and dives. In a flight, a particle moves a random length lambda_i with random velocity q_i; in a dive, it moves along a grain semi-perimeter l_i = pi r_i with velocity u_i, and attachment occurs with rate k (survival time t_S = 1/k). The model predicts the deposition profile and breakthrough curve.",
      "equations": [
        "x_i = x_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1}; t_i = t_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1}/q_{i-1}; t_S = infinity (flight)",
        "x_i = x_i + l_i; t_i = t_i + l_i/u_i; t_S = 1/k_i (dive)",
        "p_lambda(lambda) = < sum_{n=0}^inf delta(lambda - sum_{i=1}^n xi_i) prod_{i=1}^n alpha_i (1 - alpha_{n+1}) >",
        "L_c = n overline{lambda} = (overline{v}/(overline{r} k)) * (xi_0^2/(2 d_c))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "lambda",
        "q",
        "l",
        "u",
        "k",
        "t_S",
        "xi",
        "alpha",
        "d_c",
        "xi_0",
        "beta",
        "L_c",
        "overline{v}",
        "overline{r}"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "attachment_rate_k",
          "value": 0.00043,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "s^-1",
          "role": "Effective attachment rate in dives, inverse of survival time t_S",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 224,
              "line_end": 224,
              "support": "The value of the attachment rate k is estimated by matching the ratio of recovered mass and injected mass from the BTC signal, estimated here by k = 4.3e-4 s^-1 corresponding to a survival time t_S = 1/k = 2325 s."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "critical_distance_dc",
          "value": 0.01,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "um",
          "role": "Critical distance from grain surface below which attachment is possible; used for macroscopic DP prediction",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 217,
              "line_end": 217,
              "support": "To predict the macroscopic deposition profile, we use our model prediction with the designed pore size distribution and d_c = 0.01 um, which corresponds to the location of the energy barrier peak predicted by DLVO."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "pore_size_cutoff_xi0",
          "value": 0.063,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "mm",
          "role": "Exponential cutoff of pore throat size distribution",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 90,
              "line_end": 90,
              "support": "where beta = 0.6, xi_0 = 0.063 mm, shown in Fig. 1 d."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "pore_size_exponent_beta",
          "value": 0.6,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "Power-law exponent of pore throat size distribution",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 90,
              "line_end": 90,
              "support": "where beta = 0.6, xi_0 = 0.063 mm, shown in Fig. 1 d."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "flight_cutoff_scale_xi0_sq_over_2dc",
          "value": 0.19845,
          "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
          "unit": "mm",
          "role": "Exponential cutoff scale of flight length distribution, xi_0^2/(2 d_c) with xi_0=0.063 mm and d_c=0.01 um",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 208,
              "line_end": 208,
              "support": "For a larger number of transitions, the flight length distribution collapses to a flat profile with a characteristic exponential decay of characteristic scale xi_0^2 / 2d_c."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 148,
          "line_end": 148,
          "support": "Each trajectory is considered a 1-D sequence of segments where flights and dives occur alternately. Starting in a flight, during the i-th step a colloid moves a random length lambda_i with random velocity q_i... Each flight ends at location x_i = x_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1} and time t_i = t_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1}/q_{i-1}... Dives have been defined as the trajectory segments where a particle moves at a distance shorter than d_c from the surface of a grain with random radius r_i. The particle crosses half the grain perimeter of random length l_i = pi r_i with a random velocity u_i... The combined action of physical interception and diffusion is lumped into an effective attachment rate k, whose inverse defines the survival time t_S = 1/k."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 151,
          "line_end": 151,
          "support": "If the transport time l_i/u_i around a grain is shorter than the survival time t_S, the particle will escape the dive... Otherwise, the particle attaches to the grain, its trajectory ends at location x_i = x_i + u_i t_S at time t_i = t_i + t_S."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 153,
          "line_end": 167,
          "support": "Equation (eq:CTRW) summarizes the A-CTRW: flight step x_i = x_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1}, t_i = t_{i-1} + lambda_{i-1}/q_{i-1}, t_S = infinity; dive step x_i = x_i + l_i, t_i = t_i + l_i/u_i, t_S = 1/k_i."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 201,
          "line_end": 203,
          "support": "Equation (lambdaeq): p_lambda(lambda) = < sum_{n=0}^inf delta(lambda - sum_{i=1}^n xi_i) prod_{i=1}^n alpha_i (1 - alpha_{n+1}) >."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 247,
          "line_end": 249,
          "support": "Equation (eq:cutoff): L_c = n overline{lambda} = (overline{v}/(overline{r} k)) * (xi_0^2/(2 d_c))."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "fixed_conditions": [
    {
      "condition_id": "chip_geometry",
      "statement": "Porous medium: folded strip of width w = 3 mm, length L = 990 mm (or 970 mm in caption), thickness h = 0.05 mm, composed of non-overlapping disks with radii r in [0.06, 0.12] mm distributed as P_r(r) ~ r^{-3}, pore throat size xi power-law with exponential cutoff (beta=0.6, xi_0=0.063 mm).",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 84,
          "line_end": 90,
          "support": "The medium structure consists of non-overlapping disks representing solid and impermeable grains having random radius r in [0.06 mm < r < 0.12 mm] distributed as P_r(r) ~ r^{-3}... The distance between neighboring grains xi is power law distributed with an exponential cutoff, p_xi(xi) ~ xi^{-beta} e^{-xi/xi_0}, where beta = 0.6, xi_0 = 0.063 mm... These grains of random size and location are placed within a folded strip of width w = 3 mm... and length L = 990 mm."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 100,
          "line_end": 100,
          "support": "We fabricate the described microfluidics devices with thickness h = 0.05 mm that is similar to the average pore throat xi_0."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "flow_conditions",
      "statement": "Constant flow rate Q = 0.72 uL/min, average Darcy velocity q* = 0.16 mm/s, porosity phi = 0.46, cross-sectional area A = 0.15 mm^2, Péclet number Pe ~ 10^5, Reynolds number Re ~ 0.01.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "The flow is imposed and controlled with a Harvard Apparatus PHD-ULTRA syringe pump, which is set at a constant flow rate Q = 0.72 uL/min. It results in an average (Darcy) velocity of q* = Q/A phi = 0.16 mm/s, where A = h w = 0.15 mm^2 is the chip cross-sectional surface and phi = 0.46 is the medium porosity... Pe = overline{xi} q* / (phi D) ~ 10^5... Re = xi q* / nu ~ 0.01."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "colloid_suspension",
      "statement": "Polystyrene microspheres of diameter 1.1 um, density 1.05 g/mL, dispersed in 1:1 milliQ water and D2O mixture, ionic strength 0.5 mM, zeta potential -65.6 ± 0.6 mV, diffusion coefficient D ~ 4e-7 mm^2/s.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "The colloidal suspension is composed of polystyrene micro-spheres (diameter 1.1 um and density rho = 1.05 g/mL Thermofischer Fluoromax B100) dispersed in a density-matched 1:1 milliQ water and D2O mixture... D = 1.4 × 10^{-7} mm^2/s."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 282,
          "line_end": 282,
          "support": "The ionic strength of the suspension was fixed to 0.5 mM by adding NaCl... Zeta Potential of the colloidal suspension (zeta = -65.6 ± 0.6 mV) has been measured with the Zetasizer Nano ZS."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 297,
          "line_end": 297,
          "support": "The used polystyrene colloids of diameter 1.1 um that have a diffusion coefficient D ~ 4e-7 mm^2/s."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "injection_duration",
      "statement": "Continuous injection for about 30 hours, corresponding to elution of 20 pore volumes; total injected volume 1.3 mL, total number of particles ~2.8e7.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "Once the chip is saturated, we continuously inject a colloidal suspension for about 30 hours (corresponding to the elution of 20 pore volumes)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 279,
          "line_end": 279,
          "support": "During the overall duration of the experiment (T = 30 hours), we injected at constant flow rate Q = 0.72 ul/min a volume QT = 1.3 ml of suspension... this injected volume corresponds to a total number of about 2.8e7 particles."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "PT_experiment_conditions",
      "statement": "Particle tracking experiments performed at 100 Hz acquisition, 40 sec continuous acquisition, repeated 6 times per FoV, 3 adjacent FoVs (about 6 mm total), critical distance d_c = 2 um for flight length measurement.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 290,
          "line_end": 290,
          "support": "Our Particle Tracking (PT) is composed of two steps. First, microscopy time-lapse image acquisition at 100 Hz is performed at a given location... Due to the high frequency in acquisition rate, the time window for continuous acquisition was limited at 40 sec (thus, 4,000 pictures). After that, a new acquisition started. This process is repeated 6 times in each single FoV, before moving to the next FoV... We collected data for 3 adjacent FoV in total."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 211,
          "line_end": 211,
          "support": "Moreover, to measure p_lambda(lambda), we impose the arbitrary d_c = 2 um, which is larger than the actual critical distance below which attachment is possible (d_c = 0.01 um) but allows us to collect enough statistics."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "condition_id": "PT_simulation_conditions",
      "statement": "Particle tracking simulation: 50,000 particles integrated over velocity field from deAnnaPRF2017, simulated porous structure with pore throat size distribution p_xi(xi) ~ xi^{-beta} e^{-xi/xi_0} with beta = 0.16.",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 308,
          "line_end": 308,
          "support": "We integrate 50,000 particles' trajectories by interpolating... the velocity field v(x) at each particle location x_p... The simulated porous structure (different form the experimental one) is characterized by the pore throats size distribution p_xi(xi) ~ xi^{-beta} e^{-xi/xi_0} with beta = 0.16."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "controls": [
    {
      "name": "flow_rate_Q",
      "definition": "Constant volumetric flow rate imposed by syringe pump.",
      "unit": "uL/min",
      "minimum": 0.72,
      "maximum": 0.72,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "The flow is imposed and controlled with a Harvard Apparatus PHD-ULTRA syringe pump, which is set at a constant flow rate Q = 0.72 uL/min."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "readouts": [
    {
      "name": "deposition_profile_a_x",
      "definition": "Ratio of volume of attached particles at distance x from inlet to pore space volume at that location, a(x) = V_c(x)/V_p(x).",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Measured after 20 pore volumes (25h) of continuous injection; spatial profile along longitudinal position x.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 294,
          "line_end": 294,
          "support": "This results in the profile a(x) = V_c(x) / V_p(x) defined as the ratio between the total volume of attached particles at a distance x from the inlet (V_c(x) = N_c(x)*4/3 pi R^3) and the pore space volume detected in the images collected at that same location (V_p(x))."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 113,
          "line_end": 113,
          "support": "The deposition profile of colloids retained by the porous medium along the longitudinal position x, a(x) after eluting 20 pore volumes is shown in Fig. 2 a."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "breakthrough_curve_c_t_over_c0",
      "definition": "Normalized concentration of suspended colloids at the outlet, c(t)/c0, measured as number of colloids detected per unit volume divided by injected concentration.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Measured over time during continuous injection; complementary BTC 1 - c(t)/c0 shown in Fig. 2c.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 279,
          "line_end": 279,
          "support": "Finally, the BTC are measured as the number of colloids detected after a time t of injection per unit volume c(t), divided by the injected number of colloids per unit volume c/c0."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 304,
          "line_end": 304,
          "support": "We periodically acquire pictures in pair separated by a short time lag Delta t_2 = 2 s every 5 minutes at the outlet x = L. The comparison of 2 consecutive pictures separated by Delta t_2 allows us to detect particles that are moving and, thus, to measure the breakthrough curve (BTC) which is the concentration of the suspended colloid, normalized by the injected one, c(t)/c0."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "flight_length_distribution_p_lambda",
      "definition": "Probability density function of flight lengths lambda measured from particle tracking experiments and simulations.",
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "statistic": "Measured distribution from PT experiments over 3 FoVs (~6 mm) with d_c = 2 um; also from simulations with d_c = 1 um and 0.5 um.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 124,
          "line_end": 124,
          "support": "f) Measured flights distribution, p(Lambda), from the PT experiments performed over 3 FoVs (approx 6 mm)."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 211,
          "line_end": 211,
          "support": "Moreover, to measure p_lambda(lambda), we impose the arbitrary d_c = 2 um... The measured distribution is reported in Fig. 4 b as red circles."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "DP_hyperexponential_fit",
      "statement": "The measured deposition profile a(x) after 20 pore volumes exhibits non-exponential behavior and is fitted by a hyper-exponential function H_2(x) = p e^{-k1 x} + (1-p) e^{-k2 x}/A with p=0.6, k1=0.03, k2=0.0005.",
      "quantitative_form": "a(x) ≈ H_2(x) = p e^{-k1 x} + (1-p) e^{-k2 x}/A, with p=0.6, k1=0.03, k2=0.0005",
      "mechanism_ids": ["A_CTRW_filtration"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["deposition_profile_a_x"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chip_geometry", "flow_conditions", "colloid_suspension", "injection_duration"],
      "applicability": "Applicable to the measured deposition profile after 20 pore volumes in the described microfluidic setup.",
      "statistical_basis": "Fit to experimental data shown in Fig. 2b; no uncertainty reported.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 113,
          "line_end": 113,
          "support": "An hyper-exponential fit of the form of H_2(x) = pe^{-k1x} + (1-p)e^{-k2x}/A (red solid line), where A is a normalization factor, characterized by two characteristic scales fits relatively well the data."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 105,
          "line_end": 105,
          "support": "Hyper-exponential fit H_2(p,k1,k2) with p=0.6 and rates k1=0.03 and k2=0.0005."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "BTC_heavy_tail",
      "statement": "The complementary BTC, 1 - c(t)/c0, after t/t_PV = 1 decays with a power-law heavy tail with slope t^{-0.67}, indicating anomalous transport not captured by CFT.",
      "quantitative_form": "1 - c(t)/c0 ~ t^{-0.67} for t/t_PV > 1",
      "mechanism_ids": ["A_CTRW_filtration"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["breakthrough_curve_c_t_over_c0"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chip_geometry", "flow_conditions", "colloid_suspension", "injection_duration"],
      "applicability": "Applicable to the measured BTC after 20 pore volumes in the described microfluidic setup.",
      "statistical_basis": "Observed in experimental BTC shown in Fig. 2c; no uncertainty reported.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 105,
          "line_end": 105,
          "support": "After 1t_PV, a typical heavy tailing of the BTC signal with slope t^{-0.67} is a signature of the anomalous transport, not captured by continuum approaches as prescribed by CFT theory."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 113,
          "line_end": 113,
          "support": "Fig. 2 c shows the complementary BTC, 1-c(t)/c0: as expected, after t/t_PV = 1 it quickly decays when colloids reach the outlet. However, it deviates from the exponential decay predicted by the classical models based on dispersion since at times larger than t/t_PV= 1, a power-law like heavy tail emerges."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "flight_length_distribution_model",
      "statement": "The flight length distribution p_lambda(lambda) is given by the CTRW expression with pore size distribution and diving probability alpha_i; it exhibits a power-law for short flights and an exponential cutoff with scale xi_0^2/(2 d_c) for long flights.",
      "quantitative_form": "p_lambda(lambda) = < sum_{n=0}^inf delta(lambda - sum_{i=1}^n xi_i) prod_{i=1}^n alpha_i (1 - alpha_{n+1}) >; for large lambda, p_lambda ~ exp(-lambda / (xi_0^2/(2 d_c)))",
      "mechanism_ids": ["A_CTRW_filtration"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["flight_length_distribution_p_lambda"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chip_geometry", "PT_experiment_conditions", "PT_simulation_conditions"],
      "applicability": "Applicable to the designed porous structure with pore size distribution p_xi(xi) ~ xi^{-beta} e^{-xi/xi_0} and for critical distances d_c used in experiments and simulations.",
      "statistical_basis": "Model prediction compared to experimental and simulation data in Fig. 4b; no uncertainty reported.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 201,
          "line_end": 203,
          "support": "Equation (lambdaeq): p_lambda(lambda) = < sum_{n=0}^inf delta(lambda - sum_{i=1}^n xi_i) prod_{i=1}^n alpha_i (1 - alpha_{n+1}) >."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 208,
          "line_end": 208,
          "support": "For a larger number of transitions, the flight length distribution collapses to a flat profile with a characteristic exponential decay of characteristic scale xi_0^2 / 2d_c."
        },
        {
          "line_start": 211,
          "line_end": 211,
          "support": "The measured distribution is reported in Fig. 4 b as red circles together with the Monte-Carlo simulation of eq. (lambdaeq) (red line in Fig. 4 b), fed only with the designed pore size distribution p_xi(xi)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "DP_cutoff_equation",
      "statement": "The large-distance cutoff of the deposition profile is given by L_c = n overline{lambda} = (overline{v}/(overline{r} k)) * (xi_0^2/(2 d_c)), where n = t_S/t_D = overline{v}/(overline{r} k).",
      "quantitative_form": "L_c = (overline{v}/(overline{r} k)) * (xi_0^2/(2 d_c))",
      "mechanism_ids": ["A_CTRW_filtration"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["deposition_profile_a_x"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chip_geometry", "flow_conditions", "colloid_suspension"],
      "applicability": "Applicable to the A-CTRW model predictions for the deposition profile cutoff.",
      "statistical_basis": "Derived from model considerations; no uncertainty reported.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": ["flight_length_distribution_model"],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 245,
          "line_end": 249,
          "support": "The average number n of flight-dive phases experienced before attachment can be derived from the probability to survive n phases, which is controlled by the ratio of the survival time near a grain, t_S = 1/k, and the transport time... t_D = overline{r} / overline{u}. Thus, n = t_S / t_D = overline{v}/overline{r} k. Finally, the cut-off for the deposition profile is given by L_c = n overline{lambda} = (overline{v}/(overline{r} k)) * (xi_0^2/(2 d_c))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "DP_scaling_with_beta",
      "statement": "For pore sizes smaller than the cutoff, the deposition profile scales as x^{-beta}, where beta is the exponent of the pore size distribution.",
      "quantitative_form": "a(x) ~ x^{-beta} for x < xi_0",
      "mechanism_ids": ["A_CTRW_filtration"],
      "control_names": [],
      "readout_names": ["deposition_profile_a_x"],
      "fixed_condition_ids": ["chip_geometry", "flow_conditions", "colloid_suspension"],
      "applicability": "Applicable to the model prediction when xi_0 is much larger than the spatial domain size.",
      "statistical_basis": "Model prediction shown in Fig. 5d; no uncertainty reported.",
      "uncertainty": "source_unspecified",
      "depends_on": ["DP_cutoff_equation"],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 262,
          "line_end": 262,
          "support": "In order to probe the impact of beta on DP, we fix the value for xi_0 to be much larger than the spatial domain size, and we vary beta. Fig. 5 d shows, as expected, how changing beta changes the deposition profile as ~ x^{-beta}."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "source_ambiguities": [
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "length_L_discrepancy",
      "statement": "The paper reports the overall length L as 1 m in the introduction, 990 mm in the methods, and 970 mm in the figure caption. These values are inconsistent.",
      "claim_ids": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 78,
          "line_end": 78,
          "support": "the porous medium structure is heterogeneous with overall size L = 1 m"
        },
        {
          "line_start": 90,
          "line_end": 90,
          "support": "length L = 990 mm"
        },
        {
          "line_start": 95,
          "line_end": 95,
          "support": "the longitudinal distance between inlet (top left) and outlet (bottom right) is L = 970 mm"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "diffusion_coefficient_discrepancy",
      "statement": "The diffusion coefficient D is reported as 1.4e-7 mm^2/s in the main text and 4e-7 mm^2/s in the Methods section.",
      "claim_ids": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 111,
          "line_end": 111,
          "support": "D = 1.4 × 10^{-7} mm^2/s"
        },
        {
          "line_start": 297,
          "line_end": 297,
          "support": "diffusion coefficient D ~ 4e-7 mm^2/s"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ambiguity_id": "beta_simulation_discrepancy",
      "statement": "The pore size distribution exponent beta is 0.6 for the experimental medium, but the particle tracking simulation uses beta = 0.16.",
      "claim_ids": ["flight_length_distribution_model"],
